167	## Code Blocks
168	
169	Code blocks need explicit whitespace preservation and a max-height constraint. Without these, code runs together and long files overwhelm the page.
170	
171	### Basic Pattern
172	
173	```css
174	.code-block {
175	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
176	  font-size: 13px;
177	  line-height: 1.5;
178	  background: var(--surface);
179	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
180	  border-radius: 8px;
181	  padding: 16px;
182	  overflow-x: auto;
183	  /* CRITICAL: preserve line breaks and indentation */
184	  white-space: pre-wrap;
185	  word-break: break-word;
186	}
187	
188	/* Constrain height for long code */
189	.code-block--scroll {
190	  max-height: 400px;
191	  overflow-y: auto;
192	}
193	```
194	
195	```html
196	<pre class="code-block code-block--scroll"><code>// Your code here
197	function example() {
198	  return true;
199	}</code></pre>
200	```
201	
202	### With File Header
203	
204	```css
205	.code-file {
206	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
207	  border-radius: 8px;
208	  overflow: hidden;
209	}
210	
211	.code-file__header {
212	  display: flex;
213	  align-items: center;
214	  gap: 8px;
215	  padding: 10px 16px;
216	  background: var(--surface);
217	  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
218	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
219	  font-size: 12px;
220	  color: var(--text-dim);
221	}
222	
223	.code-file__body {
224	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
225	  font-size: 13px;
226	  line-height: 1.5;
227	  padding: 16px;
228	  background: var(--surface-elevated);
229	  white-space: pre-wrap;
230	  word-break: break-word;
231	  max-height: 500px;
232	  overflow: auto;
233	}
234	```
235	
236	```html
237	<div class="code-file">
238	  <div class="code-file__header">
239	    <span>src/extension.ts</span>
240	  </div>
241	  <pre class="code-file__body"><code>export function activate() {
242	  // ...
243	}</code></pre>
244	</div>
245	```
246	
247	### Implementation Plans: Don't Dump Full Files
248	
249	For implementation plans and architecture docs, **don't display entire source files inline**. Instead:
250	
251	1. **Show structure, not code:**
252	   ```html
253	   <div class="file-structure">
254	     <div class="file-structure__path">src/extension.ts</div>
255	     <ul class="file-structure__outline">
256	       <li><code>BOOMERANG_INSTRUCTIONS</code> — System prompt for autonomous mode</li>
257	       <li><code>clearState()</code> — Reset extension state</li>
258	       <li><code>updateStatus()</code> — Update UI status indicator</li>
259	       <li><code>/boomerang</code> command — Start autonomous task</li>
260	       <li><code>/boomerang-cancel</code> command — Cancel active task</li>
261	       <li><code>before_agent_start</code> hook — Inject instructions</li>
262	       <li><code>agent_end</code> hook — Generate summary</li>
263	     </ul>
264	   </div>
265	   ```
266	
267	2. **Use collapsible sections for full code:**
268	   ```html
269	   <details class="collapsible">
270	     <summary>Full implementation (87 lines)</summary>
271	     <pre class="code-file__body"><code>...</code></pre>
272	   </details>
273	   ```
274	
275	3. **Show key snippets only:**
276	   ```html
277	   <p>The core logic intercepts task completion:</p>
278	   <pre class="code-block"><code>pi.on("agent_end", async () => {
279	     const summary = generateSummary(workEntries);
280	     boomerangComplete = true;
281	   });</code></pre>
282	   ```
283	
284	**Anti-patterns:**
285	- Displaying full source files inline (100+ lines overwhelming the page)
286	- Code blocks without `white-space: pre-wrap` (code runs together into unreadable wall)
287	- No height constraint on long code (page becomes endless scroll)
288	
289	If someone needs the full file, put it in a collapsible section or link to it.
290	
291	## Directory Tree
292	
293	For file structures, use `<pre>` with monospace + `white-space: pre`. Tree connectors (`├──`, `└──`, `│`) only work when vertically aligned — they become noise if text wraps.
294	
295	```css
296	.dir-tree {
297	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
298	  font-size: 13px;
299	  line-height: 1.7;
300	  background: var(--surface);
301	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
302	  border-radius: 8px;
303	  padding: 16px 20px;
304	  overflow-x: auto;
305	  white-space: pre;
306	}
307	
308	.dir-tree .ann { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 11px; font-style: italic; }
309	.dir-tree .hl  { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
310	```
311	
312	```html
313	<pre class="dir-tree">my-project/
314	├── src/
315	│   ├── <span class="hl">index.ts</span>       <span class="ann">— entry point</span>
316	│   ├── services/
317	│   │   └── <span class="hl">api.py</span>     <span class="ann">(142 lines)</span>
318	│   └── utils/
319	├── tests/            <span class="ann">(14 test files)</span>
320	└── README.md</pre>
321	```
322	
323	For labeled trees, wrap in a card. For side-by-side comparisons, put two cards in a grid:
324	
325	```css
326	.dir-tree-card { border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; }
327	.dir-tree-card__header {
328	  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;
329	  padding: 10px 16px; background: var(--surface); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
330	  font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600;
331	  text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1.5px;
332	}
333	.dir-tree-card .dir-tree { border: none; border-radius: 0; }
334	
335	/* Side-by-side: two .dir-tree-card in a grid */
336	.dir-compare { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; align-items: start; }
337	@media (max-width: 900px) { .dir-compare { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
338	```
339	
340	**Never** render tree connectors inside wrapping text (`white-space: normal`), flex children, or grid items — the vertical pipes lose alignment and the hierarchy becomes unreadable.
341	
342	## Overflow Protection
343	
344	Grid and flex children default to `min-width: auto`, which prevents them from shrinking below their content width. Long text, inline code badges, and non-wrapping elements will blow out containers.
345	
346	### Global rules
347	
348	```css
349	/* Every grid/flex child must be able to shrink */
350	.grid > *, .flex > *,
351	[style*="display: grid"] > *,
352	[style*="display: flex"] > * {
353	  min-width: 0;
354	}
355	
356	/* Long text wraps instead of overflowing */
357	body {
358	  overflow-wrap: break-word;
359	}
360	```
361	
362	### Side-by-side comparison panels
363	
364	```css
365	.comparison {
366	  display: grid;
367	  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
368	  gap: 16px;
369	}
370	
371	.comparison > * {
372	  min-width: 0;
373	  overflow-wrap: break-word;
374	}
375	
376	@media (max-width: 768px) {
377	  .comparison { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
378	}
379	```
380	
381	### Never use `display: flex` on `<li>` for marker characters
382	
383	Using `display: flex` on a list item to position a `::before` marker creates an anonymous flex item for the remaining text content. That anonymous flex item gets `min-width: auto` and you **cannot** set `min-width: 0` on anonymous boxes. Lines with many inline `<code>` badges will overflow their container with no CSS fix possible.
384	
385	Use absolute positioning for markers instead:
386	
387	```css
388	/* WRONG — causes overflow with inline code badges */
389	li {
390	  display: flex;
391	  align-items: baseline;
392	  gap: 6px;
393	}
394	li::before {
395	  content: '›';
396	  flex-shrink: 0;
397	}
398	
399	/* RIGHT — text wraps normally */
400	li {
401	  padding-left: 14px;
402	  position: relative;
403	}
404	li::before {
405	  content: '›';
406	  position: absolute;
407	  left: 0;
408	}
409	```
410	
411	### List markers overlapping container borders
412	
413	By default, `list-style-position: outside` places list markers (bullets, numbers) outside the content box. When lists are inside bordered containers (cards, callout boxes), the markers can overlap or extend beyond the border.
414	
415	```css
416	/* WRONG — markers overlap container border */
417	.card ol, .card ul {
418	  padding-left: 20px;  /* Not enough for outside markers */
419	}
420	
421	/* RIGHT — use inside positioning */
422	.card ol, .card ul {
423	  list-style-position: inside;
424	}
425	
426	/* OR — adequate padding for outside markers */
427	.card ol, .card ul {
428	  padding-left: 2em;  /* ~32px gives room for markers */
429	}
430	
431	/* OR — custom markers with absolute positioning (most control) */
432	.card ol {
433	  list-style: none;
434	  padding-left: 0;
435	  counter-reset: item;
436	}
437	.card ol li {
438	  counter-increment: item;
439	  padding-left: 2em;
440	  position: relative;
441	}
442	.card ol li::before {
443	  content: counter(item) ".";
444	  position: absolute;
445	  left: 0;
446	  color: var(--accent);
447	  font-weight: 600;
448	}
449	```
450	
451	**Rule of thumb:** Any `<ol>` or `<ul>` inside a bordered container needs either `list-style-position: inside` or `padding-left: 2em` minimum. The default 20px padding is not enough for outside-positioned markers.
452	
453	## Mermaid Containers
454	
455	Mermaid diagrams have two common layout issues: they render too small to read, and they left-align in their container leaving awkward dead space (especially for narrow vertical flowcharts).
456	
457	### Centering (Required)
458	
459	Mermaid SVGs render at a fixed size based on content. Without explicit centering, they default to top-left alignment. **Always center Mermaid diagrams** — narrow vertical flowcharts look particularly bad when left-aligned in a wide container.
460	
461	```css
462	/* WRONG — diagram hugs left edge */
463	.mermaid-container {
464	  padding: 24px;
465	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
466	}
467	
468	/* RIGHT — diagram centers in container */
469	.mermaid-wrap {
470	  display: flex;
471	  justify-content: center;
472	  align-items: flex-start;  /* or center for shorter diagrams */
473	  padding: 24px;
474	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
475	}
476	```
477	
478	### Scaling Small Diagrams
479	
480	Mermaid sizes diagrams based on content, not container. Complex diagrams with many nodes render small to fit everything, leaving the text nearly unreadable. Three fixes:
481	
482	**1. Increase fontSize in themeVariables** (most effective):
483	```javascript
484	mermaid.initialize({
485	  theme: 'base',
486	  themeVariables: {
487	    fontSize: '18px',  // default is 16px, bump to 18-20px for complex diagrams
488	  }
489	});
490	```
491	
492	**2. CSS zoom** for diagrams that still render too small:
493	```css
494	.mermaid-wrap--scaled .mermaid {
495	  zoom: 1.3;
496	}
497	```
498	
499	**3. Constrain container width** so the diagram doesn't float in dead space:
500	```css
501	.mermaid-wrap--constrained {
502	  max-width: 800px;
503	  margin: 0 auto;
504	}
505	```
506	
507	**Rule of thumb:** If the diagram has 10+ nodes or the text is smaller than 12px rendered, increase fontSize to 18-20px or apply CSS zoom.
508	
509	### Zoom Controls
510	
511	Add zoom controls to every `.mermaid-wrap` container for complex diagrams.
512	
513	**Small diagrams in slides.** If a diagram has fewer than ~7 nodes with no branching, it will render tiny in a full-viewport slide container. For simple linear flows (A → B → C → D), use CSS pipeline cards instead of Mermaid — see `slide-patterns.md` "CSS Pipeline Slide." Reserve Mermaid for complex graphs where automatic edge routing is actually needed.
514	
515	### Full Pattern
516	
517	```css
518	.mermaid-wrap {
519	  position: relative;
520	  background: var(--surface);
521	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
522	  border-radius: 12px;
523	  padding: 32px 24px;
524	  overflow: auto;
525	  /* CRITICAL: center the diagram both horizontally and vertically */
526	  display: flex;
527	  justify-content: center;
528	  align-items: center;
529	  /* Prevent vertical flowcharts from compressing into unreadable thumbnails */
530	  min-height: 400px;
531	}
532	
533	/* For shorter diagrams that don't need the full height */
534	.mermaid-wrap--compact { min-height: 200px; }
535	
536	/* For very tall vertical flowcharts */
537	.mermaid-wrap--tall { min-height: 600px; }
538	
539	.zoom-controls {
540	  position: absolute;
541	  top: 8px;
542	  right: 8px;
543	  display: flex;
544	  gap: 2px;
545	  z-index: 10;
546	  background: var(--surface);
547	  border: 1px solid var(--border);
548	  border-radius: 6px;
549	  padding: 2px;
550	}
551	
552	.zoom-controls button {
553	  width: 28px;
554	  height: 28px;
555	  border: none;
556	  background: transparent;
557	  color: var(--text-dim);
558	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
559	  font-size: 14px;
560	  cursor: pointer;
561	  border-radius: 4px;
562	  display: flex;
563	  align-items: center;
564	  justify-content: center;
565	  transition: background 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
566	}
567	
568	.zoom-controls button:hover {
569	  background: var(--border);
570	  color: var(--text);
571	}
572	
573	.mermaid-wrap { cursor: grab; }
574	.mermaid-wrap.is-panning { cursor: grabbing; user-select: none; }
575	
576	/* Multi-diagram structure */
577	.diagram-shell {
578	  position: relative;
579	}
580	
581	.diagram-shell__hint {
582	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
583	  font-size: 11px;
584	  color: var(--text-dim);
585	  margin-bottom: 8px;
586	  opacity: 0.7;
587	}
588	
589	.mermaid-viewport {
590	  position: relative;
591	  overflow: hidden;
592	  width: 100%;
593	  height: 100%;
594	  min-height: 300px;
595	}
596	
597	.mermaid-canvas {
598	  position: absolute;
599	  top: 0;
600	  left: 0;
601	}
602	
603	.zoom-label {
604	  font-family: var(--font-mono);
605	  font-size: 10px;
606	  color: var(--text-dim);
607	  padding: 0 6px;
608	  white-space: nowrap;
609	}
610	```
611	
612	**How the new zoom/pan engine works:**
613	
614	The SVG is rendered into `.mermaid-canvas` which is absolutely positioned inside `.mermaid-viewport`. Zooming sets the SVG's `width` and `height` styles directly. Panning applies `transform: translate()` to the canvas. The viewport has `overflow: hidden` to clip the panned content. This approach avoids CSS `zoom` (which had cross-browser quirks) and gives precise control over the diagram's size and position.
615	
616	### HTML
617	
618	```html
619	<section class="diagram-shell">
620	  <p class="diagram-shell__hint">
621	    Ctrl/Cmd + wheel to zoom. Scroll to pan. Drag to pan when zoomed. Double-click to fit.
622	  </p>
623	  <div class="mermaid-wrap">
624	    <div class="zoom-controls">
625	      <button type="button" data-action="zoom-in" title="Zoom in">+</button>
626	      <button type="button" data-action="zoom-out" title="Zoom out">&minus;</button>
627	      <button type="button" data-action="zoom-fit" title="Smart fit">&#8634;</button>
628	      <button type="button" data-action="zoom-one" title="1:1 zoom">1:1</button>
629	      <button type="button" data-action="zoom-expand" title="Open full size">&#x26F6;</button>
630	      <span class="zoom-label">Loading...</span>
631	    </div>
632	    <div class="mermaid-viewport">
633	      <div class="mermaid mermaid-canvas"></div>
634	    </div>
635	  </div>
636	  <script type="text/plain" class="diagram-source">
637	    graph TD
638	      A --> B
639	  </script>
640	</section>
641	```
642	
643	Use one `.diagram-shell` per diagram. The source Mermaid text lives in `<script type="text/plain" class="diagram-source">`, so multiple diagrams can coexist on a page without ID collisions.
644	
645	### JavaScript
646	
647	Use a closure-based initializer. Per-diagram state lives inside `initDiagram(shell)`, while shared drag listeners stay at module scope:
648	
649	```javascript
650	const config = { /* fitPadding, zoom bounds, readabilityFloor */ };
651	const clamp = (n, lo, hi) => Math.max(lo, Math.min(hi, n));
652	let activeDrag = null;
653	
654	addEventListener('mousemove', (e) => activeDrag?.onMove(e));
655	addEventListener('mouseup', () => { activeDrag?.onEnd(); activeDrag = null; });
656	
657	function initDiagram(shell) {
658	  const wrap = shell.querySelector('.mermaid-wrap');
659	  const viewport = shell.querySelector('.mermaid-viewport');
660	  const canvas = shell.querySelector('.mermaid-canvas');
661	  const source = shell.querySelector('.diagram-source');
662	  const label = shell.querySelector('.zoom-label');
663	
664	  if (!wrap || !viewport || !canvas || !source || !label) {
665	    console.error('initDiagram: missing required elements in', shell);
666	    return;
667	  }
668	
669	  // Per-diagram state in closure
670	  let zoom = 1;
671	  let fitMode = 'contain';
672	  let panX = 0;
673	  let panY = 0;
674	  let svgW = 0;
675	  let svgH = 0;
676	
677	  async function render() {
678	    try {
679	      const code = source.textContent.trim();
680	      if (!code) {
681	        label.textContent = 'Error: Empty source';
682	        return;
683	      }
684	
685	      const id = 'diagram-' + Date.now() + '-' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8);
686	      const { svg } = await mermaid.render(id, code);
687	      canvas.innerHTML = svg;
688	
689	      // readSvgNaturalSize(svgNode) + setAdaptiveHeight() + fitDiagram()
690	      // wire controls from data-action attributes
691	      // wire wheel/drag/touch handlers scoped to this shell
692	    } catch (err) {
693	      console.error('Mermaid render failed:', err);
694	      label.textContent = 'Error: ' + (err.message || 'Render failed');
695	    }
696	  }
697	
698	  render();
699	}
700	
701	document.querySelectorAll('.diagram-shell').forEach(initDiagram);
702	```
703	
704	This pattern removes all hardcoded IDs and supports unlimited diagrams per page. For the full implementation (including smart fit, pinch zoom, and shared drag state), use `templates/mermaid-flowchart.html` as the canonical source.
